{"abstract":"This paper investigates engagement strategies for pocket-sized social robots designed to aid children in the longer term with anxiety disorders. We explore engagement strategies for a pocket-sized tactile robot designed to support children with anxiety through daily interaction. The robot delivers either synthetic emotional feedback or gamified point rewards to encourage user participation. We evaluated these strategies through two studies conducted in contrasting age groups and settings: a school-based preference study with young children, and a full-day behavioral study with university students in naturalistic environments. The study with school children (aged 6-8, n=16) indicated a preference for emotional engagement over points-based approaches. The follow up study with university students (n=14) across a full day of interactions revealed contrasting results: points-based systems produced significantly higher task accuracy (p < 0.05) and sustained performance over time. Points-based participants also rated their robot as more human-like and likable, challenging assumptions about emotional expressiveness being necessary for positive robot perception. These findings across different age groups suggest that stated preferences may not predict behavioral effectiveness, with structured reward mechanisms demonstrating superior performance outcomes despite lower user preference ratings in younger populations. This work contributes insights into age-related differences in engagement strategy effectiveness in human-robot interaction design, highlighting the importance of behavioral validation alongside preference assessment in therapeutic robotics.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828167"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Human robot interaction","Social robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"synthetic emotions and gamification exploring engagement strategies for small social robots in different age groups","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-828167","ids":["itu-9166568c-a8d0-447f-8e1b-106eacb24fa0"],"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Engagement strategies","Human-robot interaction","Social robots","Synthetic emotions,"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-115A4F948EB45D15F4D6BD73B8D79083"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9166568c-a8d0-447f-8e1b-106eacb24fa0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Synthetic emotions and Gamification: Exploring Engagement Strategies for Small Social Robots in different age-groups","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828167","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"A common denominator for most therapy treat- ments for children who suffer from an anxiety disorder is daily practice routines to learn techniques needed to overcome anxiety. However, using the techniques while experiencing anxiety can be highly challenging. This paper presents the design, implementation, and pilot study of a tactile hand-held therapeutic pocket robot “AffectaPocket”, designed to work alongside therapy as a focus object to facilitate coping during an anxiety attack. The robot does not require daily practice to be used, has a small form factor, and has been designed for children 7 to 12 years old. The pocket robot works by sensing when it is being held during anxiety and attempts to shift the child’s focus by presenting them with a simple three-note rhythm-matching game. We conducted a pilot study of the pocket robot involving four healthy children aged 7 to 11 years, and then an expanded study with 18 healthy children. Both studies aimed to assess the reliability of the robot’s sensor configuration, its design, and the effectiveness of the user tutorial. The results indicate that the morphology and sensor setup performed adequately and the tutorial process enabled the children to use the robot with little practice. This paper argues that the presented pocket robot could represent a step toward developing low-cost accessible technologies to help children suffering from anxiety disorders.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798562"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"keyword":["Anxiety","HRI","Play","Robots","Tactile","Therapeutic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward anxiety reducing therapeutic pocket robots for children","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/UR61395.2024.10597525","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798562","identifier":[{"id":"85200703809","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-de1839b5-30f5-4095-8e99-785aa93d07d8"],"keyword":["Anxiety","HRI","Play","Robots","Tactile","Therapeutic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A5EF78A78CF27574BCBD9463E348CAF3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/UR61395.2024.10597525","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ur61395.2024.10597525","isbn":["9798350361070"],"place":"New York","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings from the 2024 Conference on Ubiquitous Robots."},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85200703809"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-de1839b5-30f5-4095-8e99-785aa93d07d8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward anxiety-reducing therapeutic pocket robots for children","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798562","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A common denominator for most therapy treat- ments for children who suffer from an anxiety disorder is daily practice routines to learn techniques needed to overcome anxiety. However, applying those techniques while experiencing anxiety can be highly challenging. This paper presents the design, implementation, and pilot study of a tactile hand- held pocket robot “AffectaPocket”, designed to work alongside therapy as a focus object to facilitate coping during an anxiety attack. The robot does not require daily practice to be used, has a small form factor, and has been designed for children 7 to 12 years old. The pocket robot works by sensing when it is being held and attempts to shift the child’s focus by presenting them with a simple three-note rhythm-matching game. We conducted a pilot study of the pocket robot involving four children aged 7 to 10 years, and then a main study with 18 children aged 6 to 8 years; neither study involved children with anxiety. Both studies aimed to assess the reliability of the robot’s sensor configuration, its design, and the effectiveness of the user tutorial. The results indicate that the morphology and sensor setup performed adequately and the tutorial process enabled the children to use the robot with little practice. This work demonstrates that the presented pocket robot could represent a step toward developing low-cost accessible technologies to help children suffering from anxiety disorders.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808914"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Human robot interaction","Psychology","Robot","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward anxiety reducing pocket robots for children","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162639/Toward_anxiety_reducing_socially_assistive_therapeutic_robots-4.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-808914","ids":["itu-63bd73e8-8270-43af-bc72-5aaeb00ddaed"],"keyword":["anxiety disorder","anxiety research","human-robot interaction","psychology","robot","robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-05226110A50F48E36A22335C3EED061D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162639/Toward_anxiety_reducing_socially_assistive_therapeutic_robots-4.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-63bd73e8-8270-43af-bc72-5aaeb00ddaed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward Anxiety-Reducing Pocket Robots for Children","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808914","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Children diagnosed with anxiety disorders are taught a range of strategies to navigate situations of heightened anxiety. Techniques such as deep breathing and repetition of mantras are commonly employed, as they are known to be calming and reduce elevated heart rates. Although these strategies are often effective, their successful application relies on prior training of the children for successful use when faced with challenging situations. This paper investigates a pocket-sized companion robot designed to offer a relaxation technique requiring no prior training, with a focus on immediate impact on the user’s heart rate. The robot utilizes a tactile game to divert the user’s attention, thereby promoting relaxation. We conducted two studies with children who were not diagnosed with anxiety: a 14-day pilot study with two children (age 8) and a main study with 18 children (ages 7-8). Both studies employed a within-subjects design and focused on measuring heart rate during tactile interaction with the robot and during non-use. Interacting with the robot was found to significantly lower the study participants’ heart rate (p<0.01) compared to the nonuse condition, indicating a consistent calming effect across all participants. These results suggest that tactile companion robots have the potential to enhance the therapeutic value of relaxation techniques.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818240"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tactile comfort lowering heart rate through interactions with a pocket robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733615/Tactile_Comfort_Lowering_Heart_Rate_through_Touch_Interactions_with_a_Pocket_Robot-6.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-818240","identifier":[{"id":"85216466286","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-57c559d3-0c84-4d4a-be28-a23e4fcdbcbf"],"keyword":["robotics, anxiety, socially assistive robotics, biofeedback."],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-5808065BE8D0A8B16042F4D2BA1B7CB5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733615/Tactile_Comfort_Lowering_Heart_Rate_through_Touch_Interactions_with_a_Pocket_Robot-6.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/iros58592.2024.10802076","status":"p","title":"Proceedings from 2024 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS). IEEE, 2024."},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85216466286"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-57c559d3-0c84-4d4a-be28-a23e4fcdbcbf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Tactile Comfort: Lowering Heart Rate Through Interactions with a Pocket Robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818240","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents Affecta-context, a general framework to facilitate behavior adaptation for social robots. The framework uses information about the physical context to guide its behaviors in human-robot interactions. It consists of two parts: one that represents encountered contexts and one that learns to prioritize between behaviors through human-robot interactions. As physical contexts are encountered the framework clusters them by their measured physical properties. In each context, the framework learns to prioritize between behaviors to optimize the physical attributes of the robot’s behavior in line with its current environment and the preferences of the users it interacts with. This paper illlustrates the abilities of the Affecta-context framework by enabling a robot to autonomously learn the prioritization of discrete behaviors. This was achieved by training across 72 interactions in two different physical contexts with 6 different human test participants. The paper demonstrates the trained Affecta-context framework by verifying the robot’s ability to generalize over the input and to match its behaviors to a previously unvisited physical context.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712502"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective impact","Autonomous systems","Behaviour","Contact","Learning","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"affecta context the context guided behavior adaptation framework","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733193/Affecta-Context_-_The_Context-Guided_Behavior_Adaptation_Framework.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This Project was funded by The Independent Research Fund Denmark"],"id":"S01-000.00-712502","identifier":[{"id":"85192156819","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2126202a-b8cb-405d-81f0-98c49a376eb9"],"keyword":["Affective impact","Autonomous systems","Behaviour","Contact","Robotics","learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AB749BB7E3BD8B7900940F9BC903D3EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733193/Affecta-Context_-_The_Context-Guided_Behavior_Adaptation_Framework.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-44981-9_1","eisbn":["9783031449819"],"isbn":["9783031449802"],"pages":"3-15","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Intelligent Autonomous Systems 18 (IAS 2023)","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85192156819"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2126202a-b8cb-405d-81f0-98c49a376eb9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Affecta-Context: The Context-Guided Behavior Adaptation Framework","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712502","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"To improve user personalization of robots in social situations, robots can benefit from inferring information about the humans with whom they interact. Physical human behaviors and personality traits have previously been touted as possible sources of such information but often require complex processing or sensoring requirements. This paper investigates posing specific questions related to extrovert behaviors as an alternative source of this information. It aims to highlight significant relationships between questions aimed at behavioral reactions in specific scenarios and speech and movement attributes, obtained by a robot in a one-on-one social interaction. The paper used an experiment in which participants interacted with a robot through a non-scripted conversation. In it, the robot would gather information on the speech and movement characteristics, and estimated arousal/valence levels of the participant. The experiment was followed by a series of specific questions aimed at outlining the extroversion level of the participants. The results showed multiple significant but weak correlations (p<.05) between the recorded attributes. These include correlations between the average determined valence and the average recorded velocity of speech, between the average answer reaction time and average answer length. The results also found correlations between arousal levels, average pause duration, and the answers recorded for individual questions of the questionnaire. The results suggest that introducing specific questions in human-robot interactions can potentially be used to decrease the processing and sensor demands of robots and offer user personalization using only a limited set of sensors.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818246"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Human robot interaction","Personalization","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the art of inquiry toward robots that infer speech and movement characteristics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85184814105","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818246","identifier":[{"id":"85184814105","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-842f2b19-a7d2-4cb8-9784-6dcf9be5c4da"],"keyword":["Human-Robot Interaction","Open-ended interaction","Personalization","Robot inquiry","Robotics","Speech and movement characteristics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0E3E330D28A52C25F5BDAEDA03AFC249"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9798350327458"],"isbn":["9798350327465"],"pages":"1-8","place":"Cambridge, MA, USA","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"11th International Conference on Affective Computing and Intelligent Interaction Workshops and Demos (ACIIW)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184814105"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-842f2b19-a7d2-4cb8-9784-6dcf9be5c4da"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Art of Inquiry: Toward Robots that Infer Speech and Movement Characteristics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818246","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"For developing a novel kind of entertaining football and football training, we developed a playware ball prototype. The ball contains a microprocessor with WiFi connection and uses barometric pressure sensors and accelerometer data to track the ball movement in fast-paced football training games. The ball communicates with a tablet, on which games were developed that used the tracking of both skill level and the level of activity of the users. This kind of playware should motivate the users to play and have fun with the interactive ball. The initial findings indicate that the sensor fusion of barometric pressure data and accelerometer data proves reliable in the fast-paced setting of football games. Furthermore, the constructed prototype foam exercise ball proved a reliable container to house both the sensor and computational devices in a safe manner and could be useful for further tests on other aspects of combining gaming and physical exercise.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Leggieri, Massimiliano","Lund, Henrik Hautop"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-353799"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Lund, Henrik Hautop"],"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Football","Personal Health Technology","Playful technology","Playware"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"playware ball development of an intelligent ball","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2991/jrnal.k.201215.001","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-353799","identifier":[{"id":"85103065238","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-a4ea5808-0039-4859-affa-8f42cf455657"],"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Football","Personal health technology","Playful technology","Playware"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5029151C82D37D2B64D867C51DEF0B52"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2991/jrnal.k.201215.001","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/241910706/125949746.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"bfe9ab5c-3697-4898-ac13-b4c95d31c668","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Massimiliano","key":"leggieripau","last":"Leggieri","locid":"bc72b8cb-1c05-4e68-bf5f-b04a21651ac5","name":"Leggieri, Massimiliano","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik Hautop","key":"lundpau","last":"Lund","locid":"51100dd0-5ecf-47c4-84f7-f30f57dd6fb5","name":"Lund, Henrik Hautop","orcid":"0000-0002-0122-5258","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2991/jrnal.k.201215.001","issn":["23526386"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Robotics Networks and Artificial Life","pages":"217-221","publisher":"Atlantis Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Robotics Networks and Artificial Life","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103065238"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-a4ea5808-0039-4859-affa-8f42cf455657"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Playware Ball – Development of an Intelligent Ball","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-353799","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"When robots work in social contexts they are often required to partake in increasingly complex social interactions. This sets high requirements for their social capabilities. To participate in complex social interactions, the robots must be properly understood by the humans with whom they interact. This eﬀect can be inﬂuenced by how the robots express themselves and how they are emotionally perceived. The more robots are able to comprehend whom they are interacting with and where the interaction occurs, the more they can adapt their behaviors to diﬀerent scenarios. This adaption can potentially make it possible to optimize how they are perceived and as a result make them better equipped for handling complex social interactions. We, humans express ourselves with both verbal and nonverbal communication methods to convey how we feel, and we do this with a deep understanding of the context of the interaction and the people we interact with. To us as humans, this task is often trivial when interacting in familiar environments and with people we know. When we no longer have the advantage of familiarity with known contexts, we often have to rely on our ability to interpret the cues and signals of the immediate situation. As a path towards improving the aﬀective abilities of robots, it is crucial that we focus on the robots’ ability to understand the immediate context. Even a simple understanding of the context will allow them to adapt to both contextual changes as well as to the humans they interact with. These abilities are vital for strengthening their aﬀective impact and successfully introducing them into complex social scenarios. This dissertation reports the conduct and results of a series of experiments that aims to contribute to our understanding of how to create more impactful and context-aware aﬀective robots. The subject was investigated through robot engineering and experiments in human-robot interactions. Inspired by patterns in human-human interactions, the experimental setup outlined in this dissertation aimed at highlighting both the engineering and behavioral aspects of each human-robot interaction with the aim to strengthen the aﬀective impact of social robots. In each of our interactions, we humans often express ourselves using multiple interaction modalities. These include the way we gesture, how we move, how we speak, and even how we look. To understand the speciﬁc synergy among these interaction modalities, we designed and constructed two non-humanoid robots. The technical and behavioral implementations of the robots were veriﬁed through multiple human-robot interactions, and the results contribute to the research ﬁeld of aﬀective robots in a number of ways. The initial ﬁnding deﬁnes a model for systematically assessing and characterizing the aﬀective strengths of a robot. The model is useful both for the comparison of robots and as a guideline for roboticists in the design phase of aﬀective robots. By applying the model to existing social robots it was also found that the use of multiple interaction modalities for robots in human-robot interactions has an untapped potential for success. The second ﬁnding outline how the coordination and speciﬁc timing of a robot’s reactions in an interaction inﬂuence the robot’s aﬀective impact and alter how humans perceive it. That is, the project showed that when robots can respond to a broad variety of input types with the proper timing, humans perceive them as having a greater aﬀective impact. The current generation of social robots works fairly well in the context for which they have been designed. However, they rarely adapt their (aﬀective) behavior to the changes in the environment, and they often struggle to comprehend the social requirements of their interaction. Throughout each of our interactions, we humans regulate our behaviors by interpreting subtle cues from the people we interact with and by understanding the constraints of the places in which we interact. This may be as simple as not laughing when someone is sad or lowering our voice in a smaller space. We also adapt to the mood of the people we interact with and adjust our behaviors to the requirements of the physical context. To navigate interactions that demand such contextual comprehension, robots need a subset of the same skills. In this project, we designed a humanoid robot to facilitate autonomous context awareness through human-robot interactions combined with context-informing cues in the physical environment. We also presented a system that enables robots to adapt to diﬀerent physical contexts using immediately available sensor data in each interaction. We found that simple context awareness in robots can be facilitated using data that are easily attainable from the physical context. The system is applicable to other robots and requires only simple sensors available in most robots. Finally, we came up with a method that allows a robot to adapt to diﬀerent users based on simple sensors and through a short-duration interaction. Through our experiments, we found that the speech and movement patterns of humans gained from the initial moments of an interaction could suﬃciently be used to distinguish individual users and could potentially facilitate further user adaptation of robot behaviors. This dissertation argues for the need to shift our perspective on and approach to designing, constructing, and testing social robots, with the aim of increasing the aﬀec-tive impact of robots. The approach focuses on simultaneously using multiple simple interaction modalities to optimize how robots convey complex aﬀective information. This is in contrast to using a single but highly specialized expression modality. The approach also focuses on combining several sources of context information to gain knowledge on the circumstances of the interaction, and on adapting to such circumstances to create more impactful and believable robots. It aims to outline how simple, information obtainable from the immediate interactions between humans and robots can help the robots become more context aware and have a stronger aﬀective impact. The simple information in our experiments consisted of the physical dimensions of the test environment while the measured human attributes consisted of the speech and movement characteristics of each participant. Using such information may give robots the ability to adapt to changes in the physical context and to meet the user-speciﬁc behavioral demands of each interaction. As a future strategy, this dissertation suggests that robot designers change their perspective on when to use contextual knowledge and decrease the requirements on systems that provide contextual comprehension. Although a complete and human-like understanding of the current context may not be possible with the current technology, it is beneﬁcial to already use the available contextual information in robots, as even simplistic context information may be useful for informing aﬀective robot behaviors.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384775"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward context aware affective and impactful social robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067663/PhD_Thesis_Final_Version_Morten_Roed_Frederiksen.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384775","ids":["itu-ee19a632-9f7d-42dd-af74-cd81aac9a375"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5EF38C1F938261EDA5FEE34C39DBFAC1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067663/PhD_Thesis_Final_Version_Morten_Roed_Frederiksen.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779490543"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"183","pages":"128","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ee19a632-9f7d-42dd-af74-cd81aac9a375"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward Context-Aware, Affective, and Impactful Social Robots","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384775","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Speech characteristics have shown potential as a tool to identify personality factors in humans but usually demands longer interactions or elaborate sensor requirements. This paper presents a novel robot system that uses speech and body movement characteristics to recognize and distinguish between users-groups interacting with it through small sets of interactions. It clusters people with similar characteristics together and measures the affective impact of specific robot behaviors. The system was tested using a custom-created affec- tive robot through 36 interactions with 6 human participants aging from 11 to 70. 186 samples were collected in two different physical contexts and the similarity of the samples for each user was compared. The preliminary results indicate that the speech and movement characteristics have the potential as a tool to recognize specific users and as a guide to form user groups. This was found using only basic sensors available in most robots through a limited set of interactions. The results further highlight that there are significant differences between measurements for the same users in different physical contexts meaning that the participants move and talk differently with each context. This paper suggests combining the speech and movement characteristics with information on the physical context to gain better user adaptation in robot behaviors for future projects.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808909"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Atmospheric measurements","Particle measurements","Robot sensing systems","Speech analysis","Speech coding","Time measurement","Tools"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a minimalistic approach to user group adaptation of robot behaviors using movement and speech analysis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN50785.2021.9515540","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808909","identifier":[{"id":"85115076472","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-296fffc1-0902-479b-a436-29e42579893e"],"keyword":["Atmospheric measurements","Particle measurements","Robot sensing systems","Speech analysis","Speech coding","Time measurement","Tools"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-246ADDFCE3D46B8FBF916A35DD87B830"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109619244/A_minimalistic_approach_to_user_group_adaption.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN50785.2021.9515540","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ro-man50785.2021.9515540","eisbn":["9781665404921"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2021 30th IEEE International Conference on Robot &amp; Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115076472"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-296fffc1-0902-479b-a436-29e42579893e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Minimalistic Approach to User Group Adaptation of Robot Behaviors using Movement and Speech Analysis","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808909","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In an effort to improve how robots function in social contexts, this paper investigates if a robot that actively shares a reaction to an event with a human alters how the human perceives the robot's affective impact. To verify this, we created two different test setups. One to highlight and isolate the reaction element of affective robot expressions, and one to investigate the effects of applying specific timing delays to a robot reacting to a physical encounter with a human. The first test was conducted with two different groups (n=84) of human observers, a test group and a control group both interacting with the robot. The second test was performed with 110 participants using increasingly longer reaction delays for the robot with every ten participants. The results show a statistically significant change (p<; .05) in perceived affective impact for the robots when they react to an event shared with a human observer rather than reacting at random. The result also shows for shared physical interaction, the near-human reaction times from the robot are most appropriate for the scenario. The paper concludes that a delay time around 200ms may render the biggest impact on human observers for small-sized non-humanoid robots. It further concludes that a slightly shorter reaction time around 100ms is most effective when the goal is to make the human observers feel they made the biggest impact on the robot.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382833"],"event":{"acronym":"RO-MAN","title":"IEEE International Symposium on Robot and Human Interactive Communication"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective impact","Human robot interaction","Physical Encounter","Reaction Timing","Social robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the causality between affective impact and coordinated human robot reactions","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694758/On_the_causality_between_affective_impact_and_coordinated_human_robot_reactions.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382833","identifier":[{"id":"85095760386","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-68b89e6a-b966-4696-afa6-162211e5479c"],"keyword":["Affective Impact","Human-Robot Interaction","Physical Encounter","Reaction Timing","Social Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BBDBCBC6F95B12631539973B11D9DEE5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694758/On_the_causality_between_affective_impact_and_coordinated_human_robot_reactions.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ro-man47096.2020.9223435","eisbn":["9781728160757"],"pages":"488-494","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2020 29th IEEE International Conference on Robot and Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85095760386"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-68b89e6a-b966-4696-afa6-162211e5479c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the causality between affective impact and coordinated human-robot reactions","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382833","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This paper investigates the use of barometric pressure sensors in combination with accelerometer data as a viable way to track the activity level of players in a fast-paced exercise game using a football to motivate the participants. A fitness game was developed along with a prototype intelligent ball with the aim of tracking both the skill level and the level of activity for the users. The initial findings indicate that the sensor fusion of barometric pressure data and accelerometer data proves reliable in the fast-paced setting of football games. Furthermore, the constructed prototype foam exercise ball proved a reliable container to house both the sensor and computational devices in a safe manner and could be useful for further tests on other aspects of combining gaming and physical exercise.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Leggieri, Massimiliano","Lund, Henrik Hautop"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-348417"],"event":{"end":"2020-01-16","place":"Beppu, jp","start":"2020-01-13","title":"2020 International Conference on Artificial Life and Robotics","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Football","Personal Health Technology","Playful technology","Playware"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"playware ball initial development impressions of an intelligent ball","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85108859840","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-348417","identifier":[{"id":"000525322700005","type":"wos"},{"id":"85108859840","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-92d43b90-1d72-44f5-bdb5-6ecb330a2ae2"],"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Football","Personal Health Technology","Playful technology","Playware"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-47677959DFF397CA89527B0E55969567"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"bfe9ab5c-3697-4898-ac13-b4c95d31c668","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Massimiliano","key":"leggieripau","last":"Leggieri","locid":"bc72b8cb-1c05-4e68-bf5f-b04a21651ac5","name":"Leggieri, Massimiliano","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Hautop","key":"lundpau","last":"Lund","locid":"51100dd0-5ecf-47c4-84f7-f30f57dd6fb5","name":"Lund, Henrik Hautop","orcid":"0000-0002-0122-5258","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5954/icarob.2020.is2-3","isbn":["9784990835057"],"pages":"14-18","publisher":"ALife Robotics Co, Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2020 International Conference on Artificial Life and Robotics"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85108859840"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-92d43b90-1d72-44f5-bdb5-6ecb330a2ae2"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Playware Ball - Initial Development Impressions of an Intelligent Ball","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-348417","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Abstract—This paper investigates the specific scenario of high-intensity confrontations between humans and robots, to understand how robots can defuse the conflict. It focuses on the effectiveness of using five different affective expression modalities as main drivers for defusing the conflict. The aim is to discover any strengths or weaknesses in using each modality to mitigate the hostility that people feel towards a poorly performing robot. The defusing of the situation is accomplished by making the robot better at acknowledging the conflict and by letting it express remorse. To facilitate the tests, we used a custom affective robot in a simulated conflict situation with 105 test participants. The results show that all tested expression modalities can successfully be used to defuse the situation and convey an acknowledgment of the confrontation. The ratings were remarkably similar, but the movement modality was different (ANON p<.05) than the other modalities. The test participants also had similar affective interpretations on how impacted the robot was of the confrontation across all expression modalities. This indicates that defusing a high- intensity interaction may not demand special attention to the expression abilities of the robot, but rather require attention to the abilities of being socially aware of the situation and reacting in accordance with it.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383781"],"event":{"acronym":"IROS","title":"IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Robot conflict interaction behavior engineering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"robots can defuse high intensity conflict situations","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694792/Robots_can_defuse_high_intensity_conflict_situations.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383781","identifier":[{"id":"85102397718","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ad2e6f1a-c149-40a1-aed6-855378d3e6ae"],"keyword":["Robot, conflict, interaction, behavior, engineering"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FA49183568554A8D710D6612ADB39CB7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://ras.papercept.net/images/temp/IROS/files/0200.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694792/Robots_can_defuse_high_intensity_conflict_situations.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781728162119/20"],"pages":"11376-11382","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ad2e6f1a-c149-40a1-aed6-855378d3e6ae"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Robots can defuse high-intensity conflict situations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383781","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This paper provides a survey of the different means of expression employed by robots, to convey affective state to human recipients. The paper introduces a model of affective means (MOAM) to effectively describe and compare the emphasis on specific means, and applies it to the surveyed robots. The model-based survey shows that a majority (92\\%) of the surveyed robots contain a category with room for additional affective means, while a quarter (25.6\\%) of the robots use a single or two affective means of expression to convey affective states. The result of the survey indicates there is an under researched opportunity in exploring synergies between means of affective expression to amplify the overall affective impact of a robot.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381342"],"event":{"acronym":"IROS","title":"IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective","Expression","Robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic comparison of affective robot expression modalities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"GNU GPL","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84786769/A_Systematic_comparison_of_affective_robot_expression_modalities_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-381342","identifier":[{"id":"85081168097","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-057663ad-eb2e-4b29-a8a9-fce341eb829b"],"keyword":["Affective","Expression","Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FB6E31DB0383D5028C430B1132C993F4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"GNU GPL","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84786769/A_Systematic_comparison_of_affective_robot_expression_modalities_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781728140049"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081168097"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-057663ad-eb2e-4b29-a8a9-fce341eb829b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A systematic comparison of affective robot expression modalities","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381342","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This paper investigates the potential benefits of augmenting audio-based affective means of expression to strengthen the perceived intentions of a robot. Robots are often viewed as being simple machines with limited capabilities of communication. Changing how a robot is perceived, towards a more affective interpretation of its intentions, requires careful consideration of the means of expression available to the robot. It also requires alignment between these means to ensure they work in coordination with each other to make the robot easier to understand. As an effort to strengthen the affective interpretation of a soft robotic arm robot, we altered its overall expression by changing the available audio-based expression modalities. The system mitigated the naturally occurring noise from actuators and pneumatic systems and used a custom sound that supported the movement of the robot. The robot was tested by interacting with human observers (n=78) and was perceived as being significantly more curious, happy and less angry when augmented by audio that aligned with the naturally occurred robot sounds. The results show that the audio-based expression modality of robots is a valuable communication tool to consider augmenting when designing robots that convey affective information. Index Terms—affective robot audio expression","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808922"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Human robot interaction"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"augmenting the audio based expression modalities of a non affective robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162016/Augmenting_the_audio-based_expression_modality_of_a_non-affective_robot.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-808922","ids":["itu-b386dd6f-87a0-4f87-a878-d3f80cdf097d"],"keyword":["Affective robotics","Audio-based expression","Human-robot interaction","Perceived intentions","Robotic communication modalities"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4271492802E193590143598550F8BC7B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162016/Augmenting_the_audio-based_expression_modality_of_a_non-affective_robot.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b386dd6f-87a0-4f87-a878-d3f80cdf097d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Augmenting the Audio-Based Expression modalities of a Non-Affective robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808922","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we describe the engineering of modular playware. Constructing playware systems out of individual modules allows the system to be easily transported to be used anywhere, to easily build different bodies and brains, and to allow people to construct and combine the modules in a creative fashion. Thus the modularity helps fulfilling the Playware ABC. We exemplify the engineering challenges of the hardware, software and communication design of modules through the development of handheld modules for playful rehabilitation. The handheld modules allow for a fun and motivational training of upper extremities, and can be viewed as an extension to the Moto Tiles for lower extremity training, which have proved to be a highly successful playware for prevention and rehabilitation among seniors. The paper outlines the engineering challenges and proposed solutions to make such modular playware for playful upper extremity training.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lund, Henrik Hautop","Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Leggieri, Massimiliano"],"cuid":["S02-087.23-692149"],"event":{"end":"2019-01-25","place":"Beppu, jp","start":"2019-01-23","title":"24th International Symposium on Artificial Life and Robotics","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Personal Health Technology","Playful technology","Playware","Sensors"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"engineering modular playware","src":["dtu","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"rem","url":"https://alife-robotics.co.jp/members2019/icarob/data/html/data/IS_pdf/IS1/IS1-1-1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-087.23-692149","ids":["dtu-0c3371c3-a3df-4008-ba88-0ff803fbe9be","itu-327d48af-b282-4c0f-9148-598b0020e700"],"keyword":["Adaptive Games","Personal Health Technology","Playful technology","Playware","sensors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DFE82C94397AF695AD10D958D09F1E8E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"rem","url":"https://alife-robotics.co.jp/members2019/icarob/data/html/data/IS_pdf/IS1/IS1-1-1.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Centre for Playware","Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Copenhagen Center for Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Automation and Control"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik Hautop","key":"lundpau","last":"Lund","locid":"51100dd0-5ecf-47c4-84f7-f30f57dd6fb5","name":"Lund, Henrik Hautop","orcid":"0000-0002-0122-5258","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"bfe9ab5c-3697-4898-ac13-b4c95d31c668","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Massimiliano","key":"leggieripau","last":"Leggieri","name":"Leggieri, Massimiliano","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5954/icarob.2019.is1-1-1","eisbn":["21887829"],"issn":["21887829"],"pages":"9-13","publisher":"ALife Robotics Co, Ltd.","series":"Proceedings of International Conference on Artificial Life and Robotics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2019 International Conference on Artificial Life and Robotics","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-0c3371c3-a3df-4008-ba88-0ff803fbe9be","itu-327d48af-b282-4c0f-9148-598b0020e700"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Engineering Modular Playware","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.23-692149","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background:The extent to which experiencing a stressful life event influences breast cancer prognosis remains unknown, as the findings of the few previous epidemiological studies are inconsistent. This large population-based study examines the association between a common major life event, loss of a partner and breast cancer recurrence and all-cause mortality.Methods:N=21 213 women diagnosed with a first primary breast cancer 1994-2006, who had a cohabiting partner in the 4 years before their breast cancer diagnosis, were followed for death and recurrence in population-based registers and clinical databases. Information on education, disposable income, comorbidity and prognostic risk factors were included in Cox regression analyses.Results:Women who had lost a partner either before diagnosis or in subsequent years were not at significantly higher risk of recurrence or dying than women who had not lost a partner.Conclusion:Our results do not support the concern that experiencing a stressful life event, the loss of a partner, negatively affects prognosis of breast cancer.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Olsen, M H","Bidstrup, P E","Frederiksen, K","Rod, Naja Hulvej","Grønbaek, M","Dalton, S O","Johansen, C"],"cuid":["S03-089.18-685823"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Breast Neoplasms","Breast Neoplasms diagnosis","Denmark","Denmark epidemiology","Female","Follow Up Studies","Humans","Life Change Events","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence Local","Neoplasm Recurrence Local diagnosis","Prognosis","Registries","Risk Factors","Survival Rate"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"loss of partner and breast cancer prognosis a population based study denmark 000000001994 000000002010","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/bjc.2012.96","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-089.18-685823","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3341857","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"22433966","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84860256004","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-03f126aa-b27c-4059-8ccc-30e676658a81","ku-2df3f29a-7a9a-46db-8b44-8c6cb2f672f0","sdu-68d96dae-d2ce-4d83-8d71-f3a2fa22e589"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Breast Neoplasms","Breast Neoplasms/diagnosis","Denmark","Denmark/epidemiology","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Life Change Events","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local/diagnosis","Prognosis","Registries","Risk Factors","Survival Rate"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-34AD78DE0E5FB373E6E667F79F9E0760"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/bjc.2012.96","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Endocrinology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Danish Cancer Society"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2